Tim Bray once

27:12.640 --> 27:13.760
I am to me and said,

27:14.800 --> 27:15.040
you know,

27:15.680 --> 27:17.520
that he just didn't have time

27:17.520 --> 27:18.400
for the Gilmore gang.

27:18.400 --> 27:19.440
And, you know, that it was,

27:19.440 --> 27:20.400
you know, an hour and,

27:20.400 --> 27:21.520
you know, maybe 10 minutes,

27:21.520 --> 27:22.720
you know, whatever.

27:22.720 --> 27:24.640
And I sort of at that point

27:24.640 --> 27:26.160
made it my goal in life

27:26.160 --> 27:28.080
to make it as long as possible.

27:28.880 --> 27:33.200
And one time,

27:33.200 --> 27:34.240
we had Brendan Eich,

27:34.240 --> 27:36.800
who's the architect of Mozilla
on.

27:37.520 --> 27:39.040
And the next day,

27:41.520 --> 27:43.040
Tim calls me,

27:43.040 --> 27:44.720
doesn't even I am me,

27:44.720 --> 27:46.560
calls me to say,

27:46.560 --> 27:48.240
and he wrote this long post

27:48.240 --> 27:49.120
about, you know,

27:49.120 --> 27:50.640
obviously he'd been trapped

27:50.640 --> 27:51.840
into this discussion

27:51.840 --> 27:52.800
because it was all about

27:52.800 --> 27:54.560
stuff that he was interested in.

27:54.560 --> 27:56.560
And from that moment on,

27:56.560 --> 27:57.200
we had him.

27:57.760 --> 27:58.560
So I,

27:58.560 --> 27:59.680
we're never going to do any

27:59.680 --> 28:01.200
transcripts on the Gilmore gang.

28:01.840 --> 28:04.480
And essentially,

28:04.480 --> 28:05.520
that is the,

28:05.520 --> 28:07.040
you know, the capturing

28:07.040 --> 28:07.760
the attention,

28:08.400 --> 28:09.920
the voice as you've done

28:09.920 --> 28:11.040
and as Dave has done,

28:11.600 --> 28:11.840
you know,

28:12.960 --> 28:14.720
and as Dave Slusher

28:14.720 --> 28:16.320
and Don and Drew,

28:16.320 --> 28:18.240
I mean, the cast of characters

28:18.240 --> 28:20.320
is already beyond

28:20.880 --> 28:22.080
what we can consume,

28:23.520 --> 28:24.960
you know, and have real lives.

28:25.680 --> 28:28.320
So, you know,

28:28.320 --> 28:29.520
I think we're now we're poised

28:29.520 --> 28:30.880
for the next generation of this,

28:30.880 --> 28:32.320
which is where we figure out

28:32.320 --> 28:34.160
how to be able to mine attention

28:34.720 --> 28:36.880
and tag

28:36.880 --> 28:38.240
and provide metadata

28:38.240 --> 28:39.440
on these feeds.

28:39.440 --> 28:40.960
Because from my perspective,

28:40.960 --> 28:41.840
and I know this is,

28:43.120 --> 28:43.920
you know,

28:43.920 --> 28:44.880
may not be,

28:44.880 --> 28:45.680
I know there are people

28:45.680 --> 28:46.320
like Dave Weiner

28:46.320 --> 28:47.200
who disagree with me,

28:48.000 --> 28:48.880
I think.

28:48.880 --> 28:52.160
But I see the blogosphere as

28:52.800 --> 28:56.800
an annotation for the

28:56.800 --> 28:57.680
for the podosphere.

28:59.600 --> 29:00.400
I think that's where the

29:00.400 --> 29:01.760
metadata is going to go.

29:01.760 --> 29:02.720
I think that the,

29:02.720 --> 29:04.400
you know, the information

29:04.400 --> 29:06.000
that is in the blogosphere

29:06.560 --> 29:08.720
is going to be used

29:08.720 --> 29:10.960
to reveal the opaque

29:11.680 --> 29:13.280
network of the podosphere

29:13.280 --> 29:15.040
that is emerging here,

29:15.040 --> 29:15.680
you know,

29:15.680 --> 29:16.960
how you find out about

29:16.960 --> 29:18.080
what is interesting,

29:18.080 --> 29:19.520
who is interested in what,

29:20.080 --> 29:21.120
the kind of conversation

29:21.120 --> 29:21.840
that you're having

29:22.720 --> 29:24.400
in the podosphere

29:24.400 --> 29:25.760
between Dave Slusher

29:25.760 --> 29:27.440
and Don and Drew

29:27.440 --> 29:28.240
and all the people

29:28.240 --> 29:30.080
that you're putting

29:30.080 --> 29:30.720
on your show

29:30.720 --> 29:31.840
for the first time.

29:31.840 --> 29:33.600
You're the Alan Freed

29:33.600 --> 29:35.760
of podcasting.

29:35.760 --> 29:36.800
Is that a compliment, Steve?

29:37.680 --> 29:39.120
Well, we'll talk about that
later.

29:40.240 --> 29:40.480
You know,

29:40.480 --> 29:41.920
that's an old reference,

29:41.920 --> 29:42.880
but Alan Freed was,

29:42.880 --> 29:43.120
you know,

29:43.120 --> 29:44.000
the sort of the

29:44.000 --> 29:45.600
considered to be the reason

29:45.600 --> 29:46.640
that the Rock and Roll

29:46.640 --> 29:48.320
Hall of Fame is in Cleveland

29:48.320 --> 29:49.920
is because Alan Freed

29:49.920 --> 29:50.480
was born

29:50.560 --> 29:51.200
and grew up there.

29:52.080 --> 29:53.760
So this was a disc jockey who

29:55.440 --> 29:56.640
basically promoted

29:56.640 --> 29:58.880
a bunch of very small bands

29:58.880 --> 29:59.280
into,

30:00.160 --> 30:00.480
you know,

30:00.480 --> 30:01.600
into a revolution.

30:01.600 --> 30:03.680
So what we're dealing with here,

30:03.680 --> 30:04.960
I was just saying to

30:04.960 --> 30:05.760
Nick Bradbury,

30:05.760 --> 30:06.560
who's one of the,

30:06.560 --> 30:06.800
you know,

30:06.800 --> 30:08.080
the author of Feed Demon,

30:08.080 --> 30:09.120
one of which is now

30:09.120 --> 30:10.240
supporting podcasting,

30:10.240 --> 30:10.720
by the way.

30:10.720 --> 30:11.120
Right on.

30:13.920 --> 30:14.720
And I was saying that,

30:14.720 --> 30:14.960
you know,

30:15.600 --> 30:16.160
we're listening

30:16.160 --> 30:16.800
and we're sort of

30:17.760 --> 30:18.480
thrilled to see,

30:18.480 --> 30:18.800
you know,

30:18.800 --> 30:19.680
we're in the same room

30:19.680 --> 30:21.200
with Don and Drew, you know.

30:22.240 --> 30:23.520
But seriously,

30:23.520 --> 30:25.360
this is the beginning of,

30:25.360 --> 30:26.800
we're going to look back on
this,

30:26.800 --> 30:28.000
not very long from now

30:28.000 --> 30:28.800
and realize that

30:29.360 --> 30:30.480
all of these players,

30:31.200 --> 30:32.240
all of us in this room,

30:32.240 --> 30:33.200
in this space,

30:34.480 --> 30:36.000
pardon the expression,

30:36.000 --> 30:38.720
are the next generation

30:38.720 --> 30:40.000
of the media.

30:40.000 --> 30:41.040
This is the new media

30:41.040 --> 30:42.400
and it's being born right now

30:42.400 --> 30:45.120
and this is a seriously big
wave.

30:46.560 --> 30:48.160
Is, did Michael Butler make it
in?

30:49.920 --> 30:51.200
Stand up, Michael.

30:51.200 --> 30:52.000
This is Michael Butler

30:52.000 --> 30:53.440
from the Rock and Roll Geek
Show, everybody.

30:58.640 --> 31:00.000
Something that Steve said,

31:00.000 --> 31:02.240
I obviously want to lead into
music

31:02.240 --> 31:03.440
and as it relates

31:03.440 --> 31:04.800
to what we're doing with
podcasts.

31:06.080 --> 31:06.800
It's great to be

31:08.080 --> 31:08.880
in a law school,

31:09.520 --> 31:10.640
to be able to talk about that.

31:10.640 --> 31:13.600
I don't know if we need to go
deep into stuff,

31:13.600 --> 31:17.040
but Michael has been in a band

31:17.040 --> 31:18.000
for about seven years,

31:18.000 --> 31:20.000
I guess called American
Heartbreak.

31:20.000 --> 31:22.160
Yeah, I have all my CDs here for
you too, by the way.

31:23.600 --> 31:25.440
Why don't you tell us about
yourself

31:25.440 --> 31:26.960
and how you got into podcasting

31:26.960 --> 31:28.480
and what's going on?

31:29.520 --> 31:31.200
I stumbled across your site,

31:32.240 --> 31:34.560
just goofing off one day,

31:34.560 --> 31:35.520
avoiding my wife

31:36.240 --> 31:39.200
and I'm really in the doghouse

31:39.200 --> 31:40.240
right now too, by the way.

31:40.240 --> 31:41.440
I got you, you know, everything
okay?

31:42.320 --> 31:43.920
We'll see how it is when I get
home.

31:43.920 --> 31:46.080
Anyway, so I stumbled across
your site

31:46.320 --> 31:49.280
and saw that you did an audio
blog,

31:49.280 --> 31:50.640
so I was like, hey, that might
be kind of cool

31:50.640 --> 31:52.640
because I was doing a blog for a
long time

31:52.640 --> 31:55.840
and every musician probably
thinks they're a DJ

31:55.840 --> 31:57.840
and wants to turn everybody on
to their music.

31:58.880 --> 32:01.840
So I just tried it, played some
music I like,

32:01.840 --> 32:04.080
figured I'd throw my own band in
there occasionally

32:04.080 --> 32:05.840
and here it is.

32:05.840 --> 32:08.880
Has it done anything?

32:08.880 --> 32:10.400
Have you gotten any responses?

32:10.400 --> 32:11.440
Has it done anything for you

32:11.440 --> 32:12.800
or for the band or for your
music?

32:12.800 --> 32:14.800
Well, my site's been shut down a
couple of times

32:15.520 --> 32:17.760
for too much traffic, which I
guess is kind of cool.

32:19.280 --> 32:19.920
People have been,

32:19.920 --> 32:22.000
a lot more people have been
showing up to the shows,

32:22.000 --> 32:23.040
get a lot of emails.

32:24.160 --> 32:25.920
So you've been plugging me, I
appreciate that.

32:26.720 --> 32:27.440
Well, it's good stuff.

32:28.160 --> 32:28.800
All right then.

32:28.800 --> 32:32.000
And this is something that's a
conversation

32:32.000 --> 32:35.760
that's going on about, just
about music in general

32:36.320 --> 32:39.520
and are there any other radio
people here who,

32:40.480 --> 32:42.000
right there, it doesn't pipe up

32:42.000 --> 32:42.960
any time you feel like it.

32:43.360 --> 32:46.160
Radio, to me, is,

32:46.160 --> 32:47.200
I'm going to bring the mic over
to Tony,

32:48.640 --> 32:50.960
has been the soundtrack to my
life.

32:51.920 --> 32:54.320
So, whether it doesn't even have
to be music,

32:54.320 --> 32:56.400
but music really works well in
my brain.

32:59.440 --> 33:00.880
What we don't have yet

33:00.880 --> 33:03.440
or what we haven't had with what
we're coining

33:03.440 --> 33:08.480
non-RIAA music or music that
basically,

33:08.480 --> 33:11.040
that with the creator's
permission,

33:11.040 --> 33:12.320
you can play and don't have to
deal

33:12.320 --> 33:15.040
with a whole bunch of rights
organizations

33:15.040 --> 33:17.520
and payments and legalese.

33:19.120 --> 33:21.760
Is it, yeah, you can go to
garageband.com

33:21.760 --> 33:23.920
and surf through stuff and
listen to it.

33:23.920 --> 33:24.560
Yeah, you can go.

33:25.280 --> 33:26.480
There's just a million places

33:26.480 --> 33:28.000
with great independent music.

33:29.360 --> 33:32.080
But to have someone you listen
to

33:32.080 --> 33:33.280
because you like the show

33:33.280 --> 33:35.120
or you trust them or whatever
you feel,

33:35.120 --> 33:36.400
to say, oh, and by the way,

33:36.400 --> 33:38.240
here's American Heartbreak

33:39.440 --> 33:40.800
or here's Mo Jones.

33:40.800 --> 33:42.640
I know I've sold at least one CD

33:42.640 --> 33:44.400
to Steve Gilmore of Mo Jones.

33:45.520 --> 33:47.440
It becomes a whole different
feeling

33:47.440 --> 33:49.120
and it's like your friend

33:49.120 --> 33:50.720
is recommending something to
you.

33:51.600 --> 33:52.560
It's almost intangible,

33:52.560 --> 33:54.640
but it's starting to work so
well,

33:54.640 --> 33:56.080
in fact, that my daughter,

33:56.080 --> 33:58.400
who's a weblogger, she's 14,

33:58.400 --> 34:00.000
she's a big fan of Good
Charlotte.

34:00.000 --> 34:01.680
She has a, her weblog is

34:01.680 --> 34:03.360
land of the young and the
hopeless.com.

34:05.440 --> 34:08.720
And the opening band for the
Good Charlotte tour

34:08.720 --> 34:10.400
is an unsigned band

34:10.400 --> 34:11.280
called Three Quarters.

34:12.000 --> 34:13.920
And she sent me the MP3.

34:13.920 --> 34:15.520
She's not a podcaster yet,

34:15.520 --> 34:16.800
but she said, hey dad,

34:16.800 --> 34:18.080
you know, I'm talking to their
manager

34:18.080 --> 34:19.600
and you got to play this on your
show.

34:21.760 --> 34:22.720
And think about what,

34:22.720 --> 34:23.760
and I'm going to,

34:23.760 --> 34:24.720
you know, even if it sucks,

34:24.720 --> 34:25.840
I'm going to play it.

34:25.840 --> 34:28.880
But this really important,

34:30.240 --> 34:31.280
that's how hits are born.

34:31.280 --> 34:33.680
That's how music gets out there.

34:33.680 --> 34:35.520
In the top 40 radio,

34:35.520 --> 34:36.720
you know, you want to make sure

34:36.720 --> 34:38.320
that Z100 in New York

34:38.320 --> 34:39.200
adds the record

34:39.200 --> 34:40.480
to their playlist rotation

34:40.480 --> 34:41.520
at the same time

34:41.520 --> 34:43.040
KISS FM Los Angeles does it,

34:43.040 --> 34:43.840
you know, because then,

34:43.840 --> 34:45.120
of course, you'll pour some
marketing

34:45.120 --> 34:46.800
on top and everyone's hearing it

34:46.800 --> 34:48.240
and it starts to all gel
together.

34:48.800 --> 34:49.200
Tony?

34:49.760 --> 34:52.320
I just wanted to ask you a
question, Adam,

34:52.320 --> 34:53.040
because I think,

34:53.520 --> 34:55.360
you know, I'm thinking about
this idea of,

34:55.360 --> 34:58.560
well, users here really means
producers,

34:58.560 --> 35:01.040
you know, the users of all this
technology

35:01.040 --> 35:03.360
of people who want to make a
podcast.

35:03.920 --> 35:05.760
And almost every,

35:05.760 --> 35:07.760
for almost every podcaster I've
spoken with,

35:07.840 --> 35:11.920
the road has led to you as the
podcaster,

35:11.920 --> 35:14.720
because you sort of
anthologized.

35:14.720 --> 35:16.560
Among other things, you set a
tone,

35:16.560 --> 35:18.080
you said, okay, this is going to
be really personal.

35:18.080 --> 35:18.880
It's going to be about me.

35:18.880 --> 35:20.320
It's going to be about my
relationship

35:20.320 --> 35:22.160
with my wife and my kid.

35:22.160 --> 35:24.240
It's going to be about my doing
it out of my house.

35:25.040 --> 35:26.400
It's going to be about me
standing up

35:26.400 --> 35:28.000
and walking around while I'm
doing this

35:28.000 --> 35:30.400
so that there was a very low
buttock factor,

35:30.400 --> 35:32.560
you know, as well in the
broadcast.

35:32.560 --> 35:33.280
You set a tone,

35:33.280 --> 35:34.080
but you also said,

35:34.080 --> 35:35.920
I'm going to collect what you're
sounding like

35:35.920 --> 35:37.040
and play it back to you.

35:37.760 --> 35:42.000
So you made everybody feel that
there was something

35:42.000 --> 35:44.960
that was possible that they
could do

35:44.960 --> 35:46.640
that would make a personal
connection.

35:46.640 --> 35:48.080
It's been very, very personal.

35:48.080 --> 35:49.840
And as someone from public
radio,

35:49.840 --> 35:53.040
I'm really trying to think about
this personal side.

35:53.040 --> 35:57.600
Do you think that there is
something about podcasting

35:57.600 --> 36:01.520
that is going in a direction
that is really different

36:02.720 --> 36:04.720
in terms of the kind of
relationship

36:04.800 --> 36:07.920
that listeners want to form with
a podcast

36:07.920 --> 36:10.160
that public radio should be
looking at?

36:10.160 --> 36:13.680
We see ourselves as content
providers for thoughtful people,

36:13.680 --> 36:15.280
but we may not be thinking
enough

36:15.280 --> 36:18.320
in terms of actually making
direct connections

36:18.320 --> 36:20.240
with people and having people
want

36:20.240 --> 36:22.480
to connect with the people in
public radio.

36:24.000 --> 36:25.760
I yield my time to the
distinguished gentleman

36:25.760 --> 36:26.640
from Seattle.

36:28.560 --> 36:29.200
That was good.

36:29.760 --> 36:30.320
That was good.

36:31.600 --> 36:34.160
That's like saying that it's
like a blog.

36:34.880 --> 36:37.520
I mean, Adam, and it's also like
saying it's like MTV.

36:37.520 --> 36:39.520
That's what Adam did on MTV,
right?

36:39.520 --> 36:42.640
So he would get the stars would
come and wearing knee pads

36:42.640 --> 36:44.640
to try to get them to play their
song.

36:47.840 --> 36:49.600
He gets that and he does it.

36:50.880 --> 36:52.080
That's just his nature.

36:54.080 --> 36:56.880
And to Steve, it is just like a
blog.

36:57.600 --> 36:59.600
I don't see that there's a
difference.

36:59.600 --> 37:00.960
I do a morning coffee notes.

37:00.960 --> 37:03.840
I'm a writer and I'm a talker.

37:04.160 --> 37:04.960
Both.

37:04.960 --> 37:06.880
And you get a different story

37:06.880 --> 37:08.480
when it comes out of the mouth

37:08.480 --> 37:11.120
than you do when it comes out of
the fingers, right?

37:11.920 --> 37:15.120
But I've gone back to writing
morning coffee notes now.

37:15.120 --> 37:17.040
And sometimes I feel like
writing

37:17.040 --> 37:18.400
and sometimes I feel like
talking.

37:18.400 --> 37:22.160
And I sort of just go with the
flow, whatever.

37:22.160 --> 37:24.960
And so I don't see one as
annotating the other.

37:24.960 --> 37:26.800
And so when Tony, you say that
it could

37:27.840 --> 37:30.880
fertilize public radio, of
course it could.

37:31.840 --> 37:34.000
And that's like saying that the
New York Times

37:34.000 --> 37:35.040
should be a weblog.

37:35.040 --> 37:36.880
And the New York Times should be
a weblog.

37:36.880 --> 37:39.040
And the New York Times will be a
weblog

37:39.040 --> 37:40.000
at some point in the future.

37:40.880 --> 37:44.720
Why doesn't the New York Times
have an archive?

37:44.720 --> 37:47.040
Why can't I see what the front
page

37:47.040 --> 37:48.960
of the New York Times website
looked like

37:48.960 --> 37:50.000
on a specific date?

37:50.960 --> 37:53.120
Is there any sense to not having
that feature?

37:53.120 --> 37:54.400
Why wouldn't they want to have
it?

37:55.760 --> 37:56.960
But yet they don't...

37:58.000 --> 38:00.080
There's some reluctance to
actually

38:00.080 --> 38:02.240
take an idea from outside of
their...

38:02.240 --> 38:04.080
You know, they're sort of...

38:04.080 --> 38:06.320
They're very insular and they
want those ideas

38:06.320 --> 38:07.680
to come self-generated.

38:07.680 --> 38:09.280
But they'll do it at some point.

38:09.280 --> 38:10.880
They'll also put stories up in
reverse

38:10.880 --> 38:12.560
chronological order because it's
a very

38:12.560 --> 38:14.880
sensible way to put up stories,
right?

38:14.880 --> 38:17.440
So, yeah, you will distribute
programs

38:17.440 --> 38:19.920
that are created in amateur
space.

38:19.920 --> 38:22.560
You pretty kind of already do in
some way.

38:24.160 --> 38:27.200
And just the same way that a
reporter

38:27.280 --> 38:30.480
for NPR or a reporter for a
newspaper

38:31.280 --> 38:34.320
quotes people that are experts
on what they're doing.

38:34.320 --> 38:37.120
It's just the same idea applied
to a different medium.

38:37.120 --> 38:39.680
And I'd say that one other
comment

38:39.680 --> 38:43.040
that is all going so much faster
with podcasting

38:43.040 --> 38:47.040
because we don't have to wait
for the traditional media

38:47.040 --> 38:50.320
to figure out what we're doing
to carry our message

38:50.320 --> 38:51.680
because we can do it ourselves

38:51.680 --> 38:52.640
because we already have one,

38:52.640 --> 38:54.160
which is called the blogosphere.

38:54.160 --> 38:57.040
And the blogosphere carries the
message of podcasting

38:57.040 --> 38:59.840
and so this thing bootstraps up
in one tenth,

38:59.840 --> 39:01.440
one one hundredth the time

39:01.440 --> 39:03.280
that the blogosphere itself
bootstrapped up

39:03.280 --> 39:04.800
because we already get it.

39:04.800 --> 39:06.000
We already understand

39:06.000 --> 39:08.880
that the voice of an individual
